{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Hecke.Hoefsmit","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Hecke.Hoefsmit","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.Algebra","lean:module:QOU.Hecke.Wedderburn","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Charpoly.Basic"],"declarations":21,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.HoefsmitDistinguishesWordSensitivity","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.HoefsmitDistinguishesWordSensitivity","kind":"def","line":183,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L183-L222","doc":"The block-trace of the Hoefsmit representation at partition `λ`\nrestricted to a Hecke generator gives the (q = 1 limit of) the\nMurnaghan–Nakayama character; the q-deformation enters via the\naxial-distance ratios in the matrix entries.\n\nThe Markov trace `tr_M` decomposes through the Hoefsmit forms via\nthe Wedderburn–Artin sum\n  `tr_M(x) = Σ_λ y_λ · χ_λ(x)`\nwhere `χ_λ(x) = Tr(ρ_λ(x))` is the Hoefsmit-matrix trace. For\natomic braids the per-partition character `χ_λ(β_atom)` is what\ndistinguishes ³He from ³H even when their permutation cycle types\nagree: in the q=1 limit (MN) they are equal, but at finite q the\naxial-distance dependence of the Hoefsmit matrices produces\ndifferent traces.\n\n**Word-sensitivity, named as a `Prop`.** The substantive content —\nthat the Hoefsmit forms separate mirror nuclei (e.g. ³He vs ³H) even\nwhen their atomic-braid permutations share a cycle type — is that\n*there is an atomic braid word `w` whose Hoefsmit-matrix trace at the\ntwo partitions differs*:\n`trace (wordImage w at λ_{Z1N2}) ≠ trace (wordImage w at λ_{Z2N1})`.\nA constructive witness requires the explicit `HoefsmitRepresentation`\nmatrix entries (which live in the Python implementation\n`hecke_seminormal.py`); a Lean-side instance is a research-grade\nfollow-up.  Per pattern C of the vacuity-fix playbook we therefore\n**name** the conditional conjecture as a `def : Prop` rather than\nasserting a vacuous `True`.  The open state is honestly represented by\nthe absence of a proof — there is no self-assuming projection and no\n`: True := trivial`.\n\n- Ref: [leitner2026] prop:atom-knot-mass-derivation §\"Word sensitivity\"\n- Ref: [hoefsmit1974] https://search.proquest.com/docview/302700007 (PhD thesis)\n- Ref: [mathas1999] https://doi.org/10.1090/ulect/015 §3.31 ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.HasEigenvalueRatio","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.HasEigenvalueRatio","kind":"def","line":161,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L161-L181","doc":"`HasEigenvalueRatio M r` asserts that the matrix `M` has two\nnonzero eigenvalues `μ₁`, `μ₂` (counted with algebraic multiplicity)\nwhose ratio is `r` — that is, there exists `μ ≠ 0` such that the\ncharacteristic polynomial of `M` is divisible by\n`(X − μ) · (X − r·μ)`.\n\nWhen `r ≠ 1` the two factors are coprime, so the divisibility is\nequivalent to `μ` and `r·μ` each appearing as roots (each with\nalgebraic multiplicity ≥ 1). When `r = 1` the product collapses\nto `(X − μ)²`, so divisibility correctly forces `μ` to have\nalgebraic multiplicity ≥ 2 — the multiplicity case the docstring\ndemands. (A weaker formulation in terms of two separate\n`det (M − λ • 1) = 0` conditions would be trivially satisfied at\n`r = 1` by any single nonzero eigenvalue, which is *not* the\nintended meaning.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.wordImage","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.wordImage","kind":"def","line":149,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L149-L159","doc":"Evaluate a *word* `w : List (Generator n)` through the\nHoefsmit-matrix homomorphism, producing the matrix product\n`ρ_λ(σ_{w₀}) · ρ_λ(σ_{w₁}) · … · ρ_λ(σ_{w_{ℓ-1}})`.\n\nThe empty word evaluates to the identity matrix (the unit of the\nHoefsmit-matrix monoid). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.far_commute_holds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.far_commute_holds","kind":"theorem","line":138,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L138-L138","doc":"Hoefsmit matrices satisfy far-commutativity. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.braid_holds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.braid_holds","kind":"theorem","line":133,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L133-L133","doc":"Hoefsmit matrices satisfy the braid relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.quadratic_holds","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.quadratic_holds","kind":"theorem","line":129,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L129-L129","doc":"Hoefsmit matrices satisfy the Hecke quadratic relation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.seminormal_matrix","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.seminormal_matrix","kind":"def","line":127,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L127-L127","doc":"Hoefsmit matrix for generator `σ_i`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.syt_basis","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.syt_basis","kind":"def","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L125-L125","doc":"Standard Young tableau enumeration. Size equal to `dim`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.dim_pos","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.dim_pos","kind":"theorem","line":123,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L123-L123","doc":"Specht-module dimension is positive (every partition has at\nleast the trivial SYT). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.dim","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.dim","kind":"def","line":120,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L120-L120","doc":"Specht-module dimension `f^λ`. Stored as a field rather than\ncomputed because Mathlib's hook-length API is heavy and not\nalways available. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation.mk","kind":"ctor","line":113,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L113-L113","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.HoefsmitRepresentation","kind":"class","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L94-L141","doc":"Hoefsmit seminormal representation of `H_n(q)` on the Specht module\nindexed by partition `λ`. An instance asserts:\n\n- A basis indexed by standard Young tableaux of shape `λ` (size\n  equal to `f^λ` = the hook-length number of SYT(λ)).\n- For each generator `σ_i ∈ H_n(q)`, an explicit matrix\n  `seminormal_matrix i : Mat_{f^λ × f^λ}(R)` whose entries are\n  rational functions of the axial distances of the basis tableaux.\n- The matrix family satisfies the three Hecke relations\n  (quadratic, braid, far-commutativity) — captured by the\n  conjunction of the matrix-level identities.\n\nThe `.lean` file lays out the structural typeclass; substantive\nconstruction of the matrix entries lives downstream and is a\nresearch-grade follow-up. The Python-side implementation in\n`computations/hecke_seminormal.py` carries the\narithmetic.\n","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.axialDistance","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.axialDistance","kind":"def","line":82,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L82-L92","doc":"Axial distance between integers `k` and `k+1` in a tableau:\n`d := c(cell of k+1) − c(cell of k)`. The Hoefsmit matrix entries\nfor `ρ_λ(σ_k)` are rational functions of this scalar. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.content","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.content","kind":"def","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L78-L80","doc":"Content of a cell in a Young diagram: `c(i, j) = j − i` (with\n1-indexed coordinates). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.cols_increasing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.cols_increasing","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L75-L75","doc":"Columns increase: in the same column, cells in lower rows have\nlarger filled integer. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.rows_increasing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.rows_increasing","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L71-L71","doc":"Rows increase: in the same row, cells with larger column have\nlarger filled integer. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.distinct","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.distinct","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L68-L68","doc":"Cells are pairwise distinct (every position filled exactly once). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.in_diagram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.in_diagram","kind":"theorem","line":64,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L64-L64","doc":"The cell at position `k` lies in the diagram of `lam` (= `λ`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.cells","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.cells","kind":"def","line":62,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L62-L62","doc":"Filling: which cell each integer 1..n occupies. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.mk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau.mk","kind":"ctor","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L60-L60","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Hecke.StandardYoungTableau","kind":"structure","line":56,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Hecke/Hoefsmit.lean#L56-L76","doc":"Standard Young tableau of shape `λ ⊢ n`. Carries an explicit\nfilling `cells : Fin n → ℕ × ℕ` mapping integers `1 ≤ k ≤ n` to\n`(row, column)` cell coordinates; standard-ness is the increasing\nproperty along rows and down columns. ","signatureChunk":0}]}